// Practice quiz: Global error handling
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/validation-errors-and-testing/global-error-handling

::o1 advice::What does @RestControllerAdvice provide? {
~A new HTTP server#Not quite. It does not start a server.
~Request validation by itself#Not quite. Validation is separate.
~Automatic retries#Not quite. It does not retry.
=Exception handlers shared across all controllers#Correct. It centralizes error mapping.
}

::o2 precedence::A controller and an advice both handle the same exception thrown by that controller. Which runs? {
~The advice#Not quite. Advice is checked after a local match.
=The controller local handler#Correct. It is checked before advice.
~Both#Not quite. One handler runs.
~Whichever was compiled first#Not quite. Compile order does not decide it.
}

::o2 closest match::Handlers exist for RuntimeException and for WorkshopNotFound, which extends it. WorkshopNotFound is thrown. Which runs? {
~The RuntimeException handler#Not quite. It is less specific.
~Neither#Not quite. A match exists.
=The WorkshopNotFound handler#Correct. The closest type wins.
~Both, in order#Not quite. One handler runs.
}

::o3 no leaks::Why not return a stack trace in an API error? {
~JSON cannot hold text that long#Not quite. It can.
=It exposes internals that help attackers and not clients#Correct. Keep details in server logs.
~Stack traces are always wrong#Not quite. They are accurate, which is the risk.
~HTTP forbids it#Not quite. HTTP has no such rule.
}

::o3 logging::In a catch-all error handler, where do the full details belong? {
=In the server log#Correct. Developers can then find them.
~In the response body#Not quite. Clients must not see them.
~In a browser cookie#Not quite. That sends them to the client.
~Nowhere#Not quite. You still need them to debug.
}

